od, which comprises the reigns of the Hung-wu and Yung-lo emperors (1368—1424); the middle Ming period, which lasted for about 140 years, from the middle of the fifteenth century to the middle of the sixteenth century; and finally, the late Ming period, which began during the Wan-li reign (157 3-1620). The early Ming period was characterized by detailed legislation concerning every aspect of Buddhist life. Early Ming emperors took an active interest in Buddhist scholarship and practice. The middle period saw the breakdown of all earlier Buddhist legislation. Some emperors continued to favor and patronize Buddhism, but this seems to have been done with less real conviction, and definitely with much less knowledge about Buddhism than their predecessors had shown. Buddhism in the middle Ming period is generally regarded as having been in a moribund state. The late Ming period, in contrast, saw the revival of Buddhist religious establishments under the leadership of four masters: Yiin-ch\'i Chu-hung (15 35—1615), Tzu- po Chen-k\'o (1543-1603), Han-shan Te-ch\'ing (1546-1623) and Ou-i Chih- hsii (1599-165 5).” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1055, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 966})
