rumea in Turkish, it carried traces of the remnants of the old Anatolian languages. In eastern Anatolia, a large part of the population consisted of Gregorian Armenians, Monophysite Jacobites. Further south, in the region of Mardin, were the Suriyanis, Syriac-speaking Christians.  <br> NB: "I generally agree with the listing of the 1st, 2nd, 3rd and 4th religions of the empire given here, although this will depend whether the unit of measurement is just Konya or the entire empire? I also notice that the sects of Islam have been grouped together, but Armenian Christians have been put separately to Orthodox Christians. This may be due to the focus on Anatolia, otherwise I don’t think it makes much sense. " (Michael Hope, pers. comm. to R. Ainsworth, December 2023)'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 986, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 872})
