o increase the range and force of a projectile, absent or present as a 'military technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": The following suggests that Christians were a sizeable minority beginning in the 16th century, and that they began to outnumber traditional religionists in the late 19th century.  “[In 1530 CE – 1565 CE] the territory of Kafa also received an influx of Amaro clans (i.e. Christian lineages), and, it is said, Hinaro, Bushasho (the princes of Enarea), and the Bosho, Hindata, Koijo, Gingicho, Gurabo, and Dedjewo clans came.” […] “As Beckingham and Huntingford point out, Shipenao was a leader of the people of Enarea who joined their forces with the Highland Amhara king Sarsa Dengel in the sixteenth century to fight the Bosno peoples (near present-day Janjero country). It is said that Shipenao converted to Christianity and helped spread the faith to Kafa.” […] “One of the most striking and dramatic examples of northern Ethiopian influences in Kafa is recorded by Cechhi, who in 1882 watched the Kafa festival of Mashkaro (the word Mashkaro comes from the Amharic Masqal, the feast of the cross which is observed in September). Cecchi saw the king of the Kafa partake in this royal feast and noted that the royal banners displayed […] on their mast head the symbol of the Ethiopian Orthodox Church, the Eastern rites cross Cecchi noted that aside from those Kafa people who had converted to Catholicism after 1885 when the first representative of the Mission Consolata arrived, all the people were nominal Christians. They referred to St. George, for who they had built a bare k’eto (feast house); they knew the generalized version of the Ethiopian formula, in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Ghost; they observed certain feasts by abstaining from meat and milk; and they celebrated the most common feasts of northern Ethiopia, i.e., Masqal, explained above […] “It is also remembered that he [King Gawi Nechocho 1845-1854] killed scores of Christians. Christian Shoa Amhara traders reopened the routes to Kafa at this time. They were referred to as Amaro by the Kafa and they probably started proselytizing activities among them. Ogi saw this as a threat, because he felt that Christianity would reduce the quantity of sacrifices brought to the Kafa spirit of doche, consequently affecting his own coffers. But it was not until the reign of his son Kami that this threat resulted in flagrant persecution of the Christians.” […] “This king [Kaye Sherocho 1854-1870] is more commonly remembered as Taten Kami, who in 1855, one year after his coronation, allowed the first Catholic missionaries (one Italian and one Tigre convert] to enter Kafa. In 1845 d’ Abbadie had written a letter to the Missionari Consolta in Turin, Italy, telling them of the possibilities of converting and baptizing the Kafa people and saying that the king himself was interested in seeing that his subjects became baptized. His father had been worrying about the inroads of the Ethiopic Church on Kafa, and Taten Kami felt that, with two Churches proselytizing, they would soon be at each other’s throats. King Kaye Sherocho continued to put pressure on the Ethiopic Christians, allowing another Italian priest to enter Kafa in 1859.”  '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1413, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1306})
