e Mevlevi’s were extremely well-funded, and controlled access to the tomb of Ayios Dimitrios and many other holy places in the city […] “Of all the Sufi orders in the Balkans, however, perhaps the most successful and influential were the Bektashi. They had monastic foundations everywhere and they were very closely associated with the Janissary corps, the militia of forcibly converted Christian boys which was the spearhead of the Ottoman army.”  “In the conflict-ridden atmosphere of seventeenth-century Istanbul, a specific branch of the Bayramiyye Sufi order, which was known as either the Hamzaviyye or the Irdisiyye at the time, was able to take root among Istanbul’s cultural and political elite. The order, which during the eighteenth century came to be known as the Melami branch of the Bayraiyye, transformed itself from what had been perceived as a low-class deviant Sufi movement into a spiritual brotherhood that ventured beyond the tarikat affiliation. '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1022, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 948})
