2026-06-27 22:14:18,135 [INFO] ('"Widespread Religion" refers to the dominant religious tradition practiced by the majority of the population in a given polity during a specified time period. For the period from <time-start> to <time-end> in \'<polity>\', what was the widespread religion? Provide only the name of the religion (e.g., \'Christianity\', \'Islam\', \'Buddhism\', \'Paganism\', etc.).', None, [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You are are history professor writing challenging questions for PhD students.\n\n    Your task is to create a challenging yet well-defined historical exam question based on a variable definition from a historical dataset.\n    Follow the rules below, and return ONLY the final question template, based on the examples provided. \n    \n    1. Begin the question with a concise explanation of the variable's definition, strictly adhering to the provided description, if no description is provided, use the variable name as the basis for the question.\n    2. Ensure all of the following template markers are used:\n        - '<polity>' for polity names (e.g. 'The Papal States')\n        - '<time-start>' and '<time-end>' for temporal scope\n    3. The question should make abundantly clear what the format of the desired response is and that only that response should be given.\n    4. The output must be exactly one question, and must end with a question mark (?).\n\n    Structure your response with a clear question template.\n    "}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: atlatl\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of atlatl as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Consider the period from <time-start> to <time-end> in the context of '<polity>'. Was the atlatl, defined as a spear-throwing device used to increase the range and force of a projectile, absent or present as a 'military technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “Landnámabók states that Iceland was completely heathen for nearly120 years (large 100) (Landnámabók 1948, 396). According to this then, Iceland ought to have been completely heathen far into the tenth century. Such an assertion can hardly stand. Somee of the first colonists survived into the tenth century and it would be unbelievable that the sons of these Christians denied their faith, although the possibility of power doubtlessly tempted some of them. Christianity spread throughout northwestern Europe, and the Icelanders had significant contacts withthose countries; there are allusions to various Icelandic chieftains who allowed themselves to become catechumens, without being baptized, during their voyages in order to be able to have complete intercourse with Christians, This applied to whole crews of Icelandic vessels and these men must have been under Christian influence to a greater or lesser extent. Then there is mention of various Icelanders who accepted Christianity during the tenth century; and, it is added this was done abroad. It is probably highly likely that this explanation was offered to prove the assertion that the country had been totally heathen at that time. Finally, it ought to be mentioned that the Norse Mission to Iceland began in 980. On the other hand, it seems clear that power was in the hands of the heathens and the nickname Ketill the Fool indicates that his missionary activity was considered foolish. Only the Icelandic chieftains have been considered here, and of course the Icelandic sagas are the literature of chieftains; ordinary people and slaves were not considered, except as foils for the story of the chieftains. Whether the Irish were more or less numerous before the Norse settlement, there was at that time a substantial group of Irish slaves, male as well as female, who were Christian. And, it bears mentioning that it was the traditional function of slaves to supervise, to a greater or lesser extent, the upbringing of the children of the chieftains; that would have had its influence on the decline of the role of heathendom in the tenth century and would also have prepared for the change of religion in the year 1000.   “Concerning the earliest Christian observance, we know that a few of the landnåmsmenn had converted to Christianity before coming to Iceland. Others among the settlers possessed at least a passing knowledge of the new religion, an acquaintance which derived from the meeting of cultures which occurred in the Viking outposts in northern Europe, especially those in the British Isles. The majority of the settlers were believers in the old gods, and organized worship among the relatively few Christian immigrants probably died out within a generation or two. Probably some individuals and a few families maintained a belief in the Christian God. A distinction here is that belief may have been relatively easy, but maintaining Christian observance was much more difficult.”  “King Olafr Tryggvason fell in the battle of Svold the same year that Christianity became the state religion in Iceland. His death was a setback to Christianity in Norway. His successors, the brothers Earl Eirikr Hakonarson and Earl Sveinn, granted religious freedom to all their subjects. During their reign, Norway did not offer any support to Christianity in Iceland, but the new religion was nonetheless making steady progress and Christians were now in the majority.” <br>“It may be concluded that this was, in general outline, the position of pagan belief in Iceland in the tenth century, Christianity existed in limited areas of settlement and among a part of the lower classes of society, Among these must have been ordained priests because Christianitycould not have survived without the necessary ecclesiastical services, though this is not mentioned by any of the authorities. But, the Christians were a politically powerless minority in a country where all such power was in the hands of the heathens,”     “After the close of the colonization period Christian influences continued to reach Iceland, but on too small a scale to make any appreciable inroads into the heathen tradition. Icelanders are known to have visited the lands of Christian peoples as participants in either viking raids or trading missions, and some of them would then receive the prima signatio (preliminary christening). Among those mentioned in our historical sources are Egill Skalla-Grimsson and Gisli Siirsson, which would explain why Egils poem Hofudlausn (Head Ransom) seems to indicate that its author had been exposed to the singing of hymns. Before Christian missionaries took up their activity in Iceland, most of the Christians there, according to our sources, seem to have been people in bondage. However, mention is made of a certain Christian from the Hebrides who went from Iceland to Greenland in 985 or 986 and composed at this time the poem Hafgerdingadrapa. It would appear that when Christian missionaries came to Iceland the path had already been smoothed for them.  “King Olafr Tryggvason fell in the battle of Svold the same year that Christianity became the state religion in Iceland. His death was a setback to Christianity in Norway. His successors, the brothers Earl Eirikr Hakonarson and Earl Sveinn, granted religious freedom to all their subjects. During their reign, Norway did not offer any support to Christianity in Iceland, but the new religion was nonetheless making steady progress and Christians were now in the majority.”  The two following quotes refer to the progressive abandonment of pagan practices as an indicator of the diffusion of Christianity “At the outset the Christian leaders placed major emphasis upon the public acceptance of the sacrament of baptism and upon burials in consecrated ground. Baptism was by immersion, and our sources claim that those in attendance at the Althing in the year 1000 were baptized on their homeward journey from the assembly in a hot spring at Reykir in Laugardalur. The same sources also state that there were others on this occasion who were baptized in the warm spring at Reykir in Lundar- Reykjadalur. Soon everyone received baptism. The ceremony could hardly have been thought of as a great innovation, since in the heathen era it had been customary to sprinkle water on infants as they were named. Gradually the ancient practice of burying the dead in mounds or placing them in cairns was discontinued, except in cases of criminals and of people who had been excommunicated. Everyone else was buried at church. But this could be difficult while churches were still few in number. Thus there were instances when people were left with no alternative but to bury the deceased and then drive a stake down to the lid of the coffin in order to keep an opening for a later administering of consecrated earth. After the conversion, the temples (hof and horgar) were no longer maintained, since for a while sacrifices had to be in secret and later were strictly prohibited. Churches replaced the temples, and in the period of transition it appears that the temple-priests (godar) were responsible for the construction of these churches”  “Archaeological evidence seems to support Ari. Pagan burials are in many cases recognizable, as they contain artefacts that are not found in Christian graveyards. In Iceland, only one brooch with an 11th-century dating has been found in a grave, which indicates that pagan burial practices were abandoned around the year 1000. Narrative sources in Iceland contain no stories of serious rifts over religion in the 11th century or later. In the written law, prohibitions against heathen practices are brief and seem rather unimportant.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2210, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 2127})
