le name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": The following quote describes the founding of the Almoravid jihad movement and its proponents’ mission to impose Maliki Islam. ‘By the early 1050s, a triumvirate of Ibn Yasin, Yahya b. ‘Umar and his brother Abu Bakr b. ‘Umar had emerged… This was a Lamtuna bid for hegemony over the Sanhaja as a whole, understood and legitimised as a religious mission to impose ‘correct’ Maliki Islam on religious deviants and, although the Guddala continued to resist for many years, the Masufa and Lamta soon joined the Lamtuna, creating a large Sanhaja military federation, known as the Murabitun (Almoravids).’  The following quote describes the adoption of Islam among the Berber groups of the Sahara and the widespread revival of Sunni Islam in the eleventh century. ‘All the early Arabic sources about the rise of the Almoravid movement are unanimous in stressing the superficial Islamization of the Saharan people and their religious ignorance and negligence. There were, of course, among the chiefs and leaders, some persons with a deeper knowledge of Islam, who had performed the pilgrimage to Mecca, and even some fakihs, who endeavoured to improve the religious situation of their compatriots. The first half of the eleventh century witnessed a revival of orthodox Sunni Islam in the Muslim world from the Maghrib in the west to Iran in the east.’ <br>The following quote suggests that the regions which came under the control of the Almoravids were forced to adhere to the Malikite school of Sunni Islam and former religious traditions were suppressed. ‘In the next few years Ibn Yasin proved to be not only a religious reformer and a hard warrior but also an accomplished politician. By a fine diplomacy he had achieved the peaceful submission of the Masmuda Berbers of the Atlas Mountains and, in a similar way, after prolonged negotiations, the important town of Aghmät with the whole Sus region fell under his control (in 450/1058). To cement the new alliance, Abu Bakr married Zaynab, a daughter of the chief of Aghmat and this step assured the Almoravids bloodless occupation of vast regions in southern Morocco. It goes without saying that all the various heresies and heterodoxies flourishing in this part of Morocco were extirpated and the Malikite school in its Almoravid form was introduced everywhere.’ '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1166, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1095})
