n from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": The following quotes indicate that the archaeological evidence of some ritual traditions of different Mississippian societies speaks to broadly shared or overlapping socio-religious beliefs across geographically dispersed polities and over time. Commonalities in ritual objects, aspects of burial and corresponding visual motifs suggest that it is possible to position Oneota belief systems within a longer tradition of Native American concepts of the world and human-non-human relationships. ‘Mississippian religion was a distinctive Native American belief system in eastern North America that evolved out of an ancient, continuous tradition of sacred landscapes, shamanic institutions, world renewal ceremonies, and the ritual use of fire, ceremonial pipes, medicine bundles, sacred poles, and symbolic weaponry. Mississippian people shared similar beliefs in cosmic harmony, divine aid and power, the ongoing cycle of life and death, and spiritual powers with neighboring cultures throughout much of eastern North America. Although similarities in religious practices and rituals existed throughout the Mississippian world, individual polities possessed divergent trajectories of religious thought that over time resulted in differing paths of belief and ritual. \'Above all, Mississippian people were logical, pragmatic, and rational in their religious beliefs, and their observations and thoughts about the world around them were reflected in their views of the spiritual world. Their rituals and sacred narratives embodied abstract meanings, archaic language, complex symbolism, and esoteric metaphors. The numerous and widespread Mississippian polities gave rise to a remarkable tradition of religious beliefs and practices. Their religious system flourished for more than half a millennium as a meaningful and vibrant set of beliefs. Identifying the circumstances, complexity, and nature of Mississippian religion is a major focus of current research among a number of scholars, including anthropologists, archaeologists, ethnohistorians, folklorists, and historians. Although scholars debate various points of religious belief, there is general agreement on the overall religious traditions.’ ‘Oneota and Tunican artisans recorded their cosmological conceptions on ceramic bottles and stone, leaving an important corpus of iconographic imagery. These depictions of other-than-human beings draw our attention to cosmopolitical entanglements and negotiations among artisans and ritual practitioners at a time of encroaching European colonialism. We envision such interactions as sedimented in the agency of actors who actively negotiate political, and social roles, creating opportunities for community interaction and culture contact (Nordine 2000). Here, interactions possess ritual components in which connections with otherthan-human beings are made paramount and permanent. Such cosmopolitical conversations, discourse, and negotiations among Oneota and Tunica artisan ritual practitioners reflect the mutual flows of ideas, things, and values, resulting in the creation of animistic depictions and engravings on ceramic bottles, disk pipes, human remains, stone slabs, and symbolic weaponry.’<br>The literature consulted does not suggest the presence of significiant religious minorities. Indeed, some sources suggest that non-Oneota groups were largely assimilated into Oneota culture. ‘The expansion of the Oneota cultural tradition, completed during the fifteenth century, was probably accomplished by a combination of means. Warfare was frequent around the peripheries of the Oneota tradition as it expanded, suggesting Oneota communities were displacing and/or absorbing neighboring communities. Also, local traditions may have been acculturated into the Oneota tradition as intermarriage, exchanges, and alliances facilitated the extension of Oneota practices and material culture.’'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1365, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1283})
