 A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “Alain Sinou characterizes the Huedan trade entrepot at Ouidah, and the post-1727 Dahomean Ouidah, as the cradle of Vodun. This depiction is echoed by Suzanne Blier, who suggests that the larger region encompassing the modern Republic of Benin is the place where Vodun was born. Some of the earliest documentary accounts of Vodun address religious practices in the Kingdom of Hueda dating to the mid-17th - to early 18th-century […] As for religious life in 17th-century Hueda, Mawu is chronicled as the supreme deity who was too distant for individuals to approach through direct worship.” […] “On those Vodun falling beneath Mawu in the hierarchical Huedan pantheon, late 17th- and early 18th-century European travellers described with virtual unanimity the python deity Dangbe as a primary, and the tutelary, deity for the kingdom.” […] “Bosman estimated that the Huedan pantheon contained 120,000 deities, a figure that should be viewed more as hyperbole than an actual census of cosmological actors. Nonetheless, numerous deities inhabited the Huedan pantheon.”  “The European trader Bosman (1705) suggested that Huedans evoked Dangbe often, but notably during shared interests and corporate political ceremonies. Dangbe, although a public deity, was closely associated with eh Huedan royal family.” <br>“The role of Vodun as ‘messenger’ is also an unambiguous one, yet at times the news can be good, at times bad. Vodun nonetheless always posits a ‘link, a mutual interdependence’ between the physical and cosmological worlds. In attempting to deepen our temporal understanding of the material, spatial, and socio-cultural dimensions of Vodun, this paper investigates a series of 17th- and 18th-century offering locales that […] were used by the Huedan initiates in efforts to channel and concentrate the cosmological forces of Vodun.” […] “Alain Sinou characterizes the Huedan trade entrepot at Ouidah, and the post-1727 Dahomean Ouidah, as the cradle of Vodun. This depiction is echoed by Suzanne Blier, who suggests that the larger region encompassing the modern Republic of Benin is the place where Vodun was born. Some of the earliest documentary accounts of Vodun address religious practices in the Kingdom of Hueda dating to the mid-17th - to early 18th-century […] As for religious life in 17th-century Hueda, Mawu is chronicled as the supreme deity who was too distant for individuals to approach through direct worship.” […] “On those Vodun falling beneath Mawu in the hierarchical Huedan pantheon, late 17th- and early 18th-century European travellers described with virtual unanimity the python deity Dangbe as a primary, and the tutelary, deity for the kingdom.” […] “Bosman estimated that the Huedan pantheon contained 120,000 deities, a figure that should be viewed more as hyperbole than an actual census of cosmological actors. Nonetheless, numerous deities inhabited the Huedan pantheon.” […] “Given archaeological findings from Savi countryside assemblage, it is probable that Huedans living in the 17th-and 18th-century communities surrounding Savi created and used a range of both public and private religious spaces as well.”   “Alongside their use as borrow pits, researchers argue for connections between ditches in the region of Abomey and the Savi palace zone and the Huedan python deity Dangbe, who Huedans considered to have the ability to check movement and create zones of inclusion and protection.” […] “The Atlantic trade items archaeologically recovered in the Huedan countryside were concentrated in offerings and ritual spaces. Such religious spaces and the cosmological actors they attracted maintained a fine balance between trade and cosmos.”  “The European trader Bosman (1705) suggested that Huedans evoked Dangbe often, but notably during shared interests and corporate political ceremonies. Dangbe, although a public deity, was closely associated with eh Huedan royal family.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1531, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1458})
