as a 'military technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": "Elements of the state cult were extraordinarily exclusive—only the mamakuna priestesses and the Inca interacted with the golden image of the Sun that resided in the Coricancha, whereas the Inca people of Cusco used a different object to represent the Sun during their public ceremonies, for which they expelled foreign and ritually impure people from their city.  The Incas were tolerant and generally acknowledged the supernatural force of objects, places, and bodies considered to be sacred (i.e. huacas), but that is not the same as “inclusive.”  Additionally, they inserted themselves into local shrines and pilgrimage practices strategically—in some places, they supported cult activities, but they did not intervene in the everyday offerings and prayers that farmers made to the earth (Pachamama) or fishers made to the Ocean (Mamaqocha). <br>\r\n"I’d say Inca practices at the imperial level were designed to be multivocal.  In Cusco, the Incas claimed to hold provincial huacas hostage and to beat them in times of rebellion.  In provinces like Huarochirí, local people said that the Incas were devotees of their huacas, begging them to come on military campaigns because they could not conquer without them. <br>\r\n"In Cusco specifically, Inca religion predominated, but there were dozens of non-Inca groups living there, too, and they had their own local practices.  The description of Cusco’s shrine (ceque) network in Cobo’s 1653 chronicle makes it clear that, although Inca nobles and other Cusco elites promoted the city and its Coricancha temple as the center of a network in which they offered sacrifices, many of those shrines belonged to local non-Inca people:  springs where they sacrificed to ensure irrigation water, outcrops where they kept ancestral mummies, etc.  I think “vast majority” is apt, but even in the imperial heartland, “Inca religion” did not apply in all ritual contexts." (R. Alan Covey, pers. comm. to R. Ainsworth, June 2023)'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1126, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1024})
