set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “For religious observances the Yakuts had shamans, whose costume, tambourines and practices were very similar to those of the Tungus. Unlike the Tungus, the Yakuts believed not only in ubiquitous spirits of natural phenomena, but also in a pantheon of gods with Uluu-toyon (‘great chief’) at their head. In this they showed their affinity with the Turkic peoples of the Altai-Sayan region. In common with other Siberian peoples the Yakuts revered wild animals, in particular, the bear, the eagle, the raven, and as an equestrian people it was natural that they used horses as victims for sacrificial offerings to the spirits of the ‘upper world’.”  “Pre-Christian beliefs comprise the dominant component of the traditional worldview of the Yakuts. “Traditional” because the foundation for such a worldview by these people was developed during an era when mythological-epic thinking dominated among a people of a preclass society (L’vova et al., p. 6). “World-view” is interpreted here to mean “the spiritual assimilation of the universe . . . from the point of view of the interrelation of such fundamental elements of the universe as nature and humanity” (Chanyshev, p. 38)  The following quotes are based on an account given by a Swedish writer in the 18th century named Starhlenberg. I am unsure if it appropriate to infer aspects of his account are applicable to earlier practice. “As regards the Yakut, Strahlenberg says that they sacrifice to an invisible God in heave, of whom they have made an image, hung upon a tree and surrounded with furs. There are also sacred trees among the Yakut, adorned with iron, brass and copper ornaments. The Yakut shamans use drums and wear a special costume, to which are attached bits of iron, rattles, and bells. At the New Year festival, in April, the Yakut sacrifice horses and oxen and sprinkle qumis in the air and on a special fire. The Yakut funeral customs vary. Leading members of the community are buried beneath imposing trees, but most people are just abandoned in their huts when they die. Each Yakut tribe has a totem bird or animal which they must not eat.”  The following quote refers to German natural scientist Johann Georg Gmelin who travelled to Siberia between  1733-1743. “In Gmelin’s account we are further told of the various being venerated by the Yakut in one of their ceremonies: the shaman officiating prays to gods, demons, dead shamans, important places, rivers, lakes, woods, rocks, and the earth and fire and invokes by name 22 beings in all.68”  The following quotes are from an account published in 1802, and as such, the letter ‘f’ replaces the letter ‘s’ in many words. The following description related to the spring/summer ceremonies centred on mares milk. “When the hut is full, the elder fhaman rifes, and commands one of the Socha that he knows to be qualified (namely that has not feen a corfpe within the month, and that has never been accused of theft, or bearing falfe witness againft any body, which defiles them forever, and renders them unqualified for this facred and folemn task) to take a large goblet, called a tfhoron, which is ufed to drink out of on folemn occasions, and fill it with koumis out of the firft fymir… [long description of ceremony]… The elder fhaman then, taking the tfhoron, drinks a little, and hands it to his brethren of the fame order; from whom it paffes to the company as they fit, except fuch are defiled. Women are not admitted into the hut; nor are they, or the disqualified, allowed any of the koumis out of the firft fymir, which they call fantified, as poffeffing the power of purifying and strengthening in a divine fenfe.” <br>As religion was entwined with Sakha culture, society, and history, I am inclined to think that all members of the community would be raised in their traditional spiritual practices. There is no mention of competing religious/spiritual practices among the Sakha in this period in the sources consulted, however, it is inferred as none of the sources consulted explicitly address this.'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1603, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1512})
