ilitary technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": ‘What we know of traditional world view derives largely from the work of German ethnographers before and just after World War I. It has been possible to verify some of their work in studies undertaken since World War II, but only in part; and it has been possible to add very little’ (Goodenough 2002:83)  ‘As traditionally conceived the cosmos consisted of two major spirit realms, divided by the surface of the earth and sea. Mortal humans loved and worked on the dividing surface at the interface of both realms, which were inhabited by a variety of spirits and gods. Spirits and demons shared the dividing surface with humans, as well, and were likely to be encountered anywhere people did not have their habitations. The spirit realms were the source of vital power and effective will (manaman) from which all forms of life derived their existence. As one went away from one’s own home island, one moved progressively nearer  to this spirit world and finally entered it. Very distant places were places in the sky. Thus things that came from far away came from the spirit world. They included driftwood logs (in legend the canoe of spirits), pumice stone and the Portuguese man-of-war. They also included people who drifted in from unknown places far away bringing with them new crafts, medicine, shells, and, occasionally, new crops or crop varieties’ (Goodenough 2002:83-84)   ‘The regions where people resided, as distinct from the sky and undersea worlds, were also well populated with spirits. They included local spirits, associated with hills, mountains, springs, and other natural features. Important among them were the ‘sharp waters’ (chénúkken), who inhabited reefs and caused illness. There were ‘spirits of soil’ (énúúnúpwpwún), who also caused illness. There were tree spirits, sea spirits, wave spirits, driftwood spirits, spirits that prayed on pregnant women. There were demons, spirits that were inherently malevolent and that were a popular subject (like ogres in European folklore) in story telling. And there were ghosts, ‘bad souls’ of the dead, especially the recent dead, that haunted the environs of the graves. The sky gods and goddesses (énúún nááng) who resided in ‘Under Brow’ were all of the class of spirits that could take complete human form (enúúyaramas ‘human spirits’. A number of them were known by the same names through the entire Chuukic speaking chain of atolls in the Caroline Islands. Some inhabitants of ‘South’ could also take human form, but most could not. Other spirits and demons that took human form were unable to do so completely, retaining some attribute by which they betrayed their identity to the wary. Some spirits and gods took possession of the bodies of living humans, either causing madness or using a person’s body as a vehicle for communicating with people. Good souls of the dead that, as ‘effecting spirits’ (énúúsór) had established relations with their living descendants through the person of a spirit medium while perched on the medium’s shoulder. People could not ordinarily see such a spirit, except in dreams, but a medium could. Mediums could send their good souls to the sky world on errands.’ (Goodenough 2002:87) '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1373, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1270})
