 century, when an extensive network of diocesan churches was already operating in Yakutia. According to the First General Census of the Population In the Russian Empire, more than 98% of the region\'s population professed Orthodoxy. But the census recorded the official religious belonging… Information about shamans, as a rule, did not leave the limits of the diocese. Traveling priests testify to the systematic activity of shamans.” (Rough translation of Russian text) <br>As mentioned in the above quotation, officially the vast majority (98%) were Orthodox, however, in practice Shamanistic rituals retained their importance in daily life. Several of the quotations in the syncretism section will better illustrate what this hybrid version of Orthodoxy and Shamanism looked like in practice.  "By the end of the 18th century, the majority of the population of the Yakutsk Territory were converted to the new Orthodox faith, but then, and later, the local population was characterized by syncretism." (Rough translation of Russian text) '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1040, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 950})
