tation of some at least of the megalithic tombs, circles, and alignments of the third and early second millennia. By the end of the millennium these monuments had been largely abandoned, and a new emphasis had developed on the deposition of items of value by burying them in pits in the ground or throwing them into watery places such as rivers, springs, and bogs. The focus on deposition intensified throughout the first millennium. This behaviour implies a more earth-related belief system which may be seen to reflect the increased emphasis now being placed on the organization of the land and its productive capacity.” <br>“The funerary record is, of course, overemphasised in the archaeological record, but evidence for ritual and religious activity is also abundant in the early Iron Age.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 963, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 861})
