came popular in Assyria. Consequently, Adad-nirari built him a temple in Ashur.”“Increasingly, Assyria and Babylonia shared the same gods and had similar rituals. Marduk was venerated in Assyrian shrines; in Babylon, a branch cult of the great Assyrian goddess of war Ishtar-of-Nineveh had long been in place.”  “Every city had its patron deity who presided over the local pantheon. […] On a local level, the multitude of Mesopotamian gods was organized according to human-style kinship and social patterns, so that patron deities were attributed with spouses, children, households, and courts. On a regional level, an overarching cultural-religious tradition strove to integrate the multitude of local gods into a hierarchical system, which comprised the supra-regional pantheon recorded in god lists and myth.” <br>“Religion may be seen as a salient feature of Babylonian culture, which could not exist independently of the culture: being a Babylonian meant believing in the Babylonian gods.”  “Every city had its patron deity who presided over the local pantheon. […] On a local level, the multitude of Mesopotamian gods was organized according to human-style kinship and social patterns, so that patron deities were attributed with spouses, children, households, and courts. On a regional level, an overarching cultural-religious tradition strove to integrate the multitude of local gods into a hierarchical system, which comprised the supra-regional pantheon recorded in god lists and myth.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1169, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1092})
